---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import TokenBudgetPlanner from '../../components/TokenBudgetPlanner.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Token Budget Planner" description="Work backward from a monthly dollar budget to how many tokens and requests you can afford.">
  <TokenBudgetPlanner client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why working backward from budget is often more useful</h2>
  <p>Most cost calculators go from usage to cost, but when you're operating under a fixed budget (a startup's runway, a feature's cost cap), the more useful question is reversed — given this budget, how much usage can I actually support?</p>

  <h2>How to use this planner</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your monthly dollar budget.</li>
    <li>Enter average tokens per request and pricing.</li>
    <li>See how many requests your budget supports per month.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not leaving margin for usage spikes</strong>, planning exactly to budget capacity and getting caught off guard by a traffic surge.</li>
    <li><strong>Using average token count without considering variance</strong> — some requests will use significantly more tokens than the average, affecting real-world budget consumption.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Setting usage limits for a feature with a fixed monthly AI budget</li>
    <li>Communicating to stakeholders how much usage a given budget actually supports</li>
    <li>Deciding whether a planned feature is financially viable within budget constraints</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Should I plan to exactly the budget limit?</strong> No — build in margin for usage variance and unexpected spikes rather than planning to exactly consume the full budget every month.</p>
</ToolLayout>
